On the existential part: the “Accepted” checkmark combines several reward mechanisms—closure after uncertainty, visible evidence of competence, intermittent reinforcement, and social comparison. Intermittent rewards are particularly good at keeping attention because failure makes the next success feel more valuable.

That does not mean spending an entire day stuck is productive. Once you are cycling through the same ideas, taking a break is usually the technically rational move, not surrender. The checkmark is an effective feedback signal, but it is not a meaningful measure of sanity, intelligence, or personal worth.

The comparison is useful, but it breaks down if taken too literally. Neural networks are loosely brain-inspired; they are not working replicas of human cognition. Both humans and models learn patterns from prior material, but that broad similarity does not make the processes—or their legal treatment—equivalent.

Copyright also does not prohibit being influenced by existing works. It generally protects particular expression, not ideas, facts, methods, or usually style. Humans can and do infringe copyright when they reproduce protected expression too closely. The live disputes around AI involve several distinct questions:

- whether copying works during training is legally permissible;
- whether training retains or reproduces protected expression rather than merely learning general patterns;
- whether particular outputs are substantially similar to existing works;
- how licensing, attribution, market substitution, and massive commercial scale should matter.

So “if AI training is infringement, then all human learning is infringement” does not necessarily follow. Human learning does not normally involve an organization making industrial-scale digital copies of millions of works, and the law can distinguish activities based on their mechanisms, purposes, and economic effects.

Emotion is also not the only relevant difference. Humans are embodied agents with intentions, needs, autobiographical memory, social relationships, moral and legal responsibility, and the ability to understand or revise a work in light of lived consequences. Current models have no independent aims or stake in what they produce; prompts, selection, interpretation, and deployment come from people and institutions.

At the same time, lack of emotion does not automatically make an output meaningless. Audiences can find meaning in accidental, procedural, or machine-generated artifacts, and human artists often use rules, chance, or emotional distance. Emotional experience may contribute to authorship and artistic depth, but it is neither a necessary condition for an object to affect people nor a complete theory of creativity.

My view is that AI generation and human creation share a real but high-level feature—both recombine learned patterns. The analogy becomes misleading when it erases differences in embodiment, agency, comprehension, responsibility, copying mechanisms, and scale. Copyright policy should therefore be argued on those concrete grounds rather than on the claim that AI either “creates exactly like humans” or merely “collages” its training data.

